ACT I — “Setting the Stage” (10:00–10:20)

INT. LORNE’S OFFICE – MONDAY MORNING – JANUARY 10, 1977 – 10:00 AM

The air is dry. Smoke clings low in the room. Coffee smells acidic. One wall is lined with sketches from last week. On the other: a dry erase board with nothing written on it yet.

LORNE MICHAELS stands near the window, flipping through a legal pad. Around him: the cast, writers, a few producers, and—seated somewhat stiffly in a wooden chair near the radiator—RALPH NADER.

There’s an invisible hole in the room. Chevy Chase isn’t there. And no one wants to be the first to say so.

LORNE (dry, composed):“Alright, good morning. For those of you who didn’t make it to rehearsal Friday—or remember it—this is Ralph Nader.”

NADER (standing just enough to acknowledge):“Good morning.”Beat.“I understand this meeting will be dangerous, untested, and mostly unregulated. I'm ready.”

Scattered polite laughter. Belushi coughs. Radner grins. Franken scribbles something into a notepad but doesn’t look up.

﻿

CURTIN (sotto):“Nice. He’s already hosting better than half our guests.”

﻿

LORNE (sits on the edge of his desk):“We’ll do the usual—go around the room. Throw whatever you’ve got. Ralph’s not a comedian, so keep it flexible.”

BELUSHI (muttering):“That’s one way to say he doesn’t fall down stairs.”

LORNE:“What was that, John?”

BELUSHI (smiling):“Nothing. Old sketch idea.”

﻿

The meeting begins. First up is GILDA RADNER. She barely clears her throat before slipping into character.

GILDA (as Emily Litella):“What’s all this fuss I keep hearing about… Puerto Rico becoming a steak? Why would anyone want to turn an island into a slab of meat? It makes no sense! Unless it’s kosher, of course…”

Laughter—not big, but familiar. A rhythm the room knows.

NADER (bemused):“Should I be concerned that I almost believed the first half of that?”

